###  http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#fear
:fear rdf:type :Emotion ;
                rdfs:label "fear induced by the visual and textual content"@en ;
                rdfs:comment "The image of a car overturned on its roof, combined with the text 'this is what happens when you don't spank your kids,' is designed to evoke fear in the viewer. It uses the visual of a dangerous accident to suggest that not using corporal punishment leads to catastrophic outcomes, thereby instilling fear in parents who do not spank their children."@en ;
                :extractionMethod "llm_extraction_huggingface" ;
                :extractedFrom "74602.png" ;
                :extractionTimestamp "2025-12-19T12:17:03.821917" .

###  http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#mapping_1
:mapping_1 rdf:type :AnalogicalMapping ;
                rdfs:label "The overturned car is mapped as the consequence of not spanking children."@en ;
                rdfs:comment "The text 'this is what happens when you don't spank your kids' explicitly maps the visual of the overturned car as the outcome or consequence of the inaction (not spanking). The car accident is used as a metaphorical representation for the perceived negative result of not using corporal punishment."@en ;
                :extractionMethod "llm_extraction_huggingface" ;
                :extractedFrom "74602.png" ;
                :extractionTimestamp "2025-12-19T12:17:03.821917" .

###  http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#semiotic_projection_1
:semiotic_projection_1 rdf:type :SemioticProjection ;
                rdfs:label "User projected onto the textual material"@en ;
                rdfs:comment "The user is projected onto the text 'this is what happens when you don't spank your kids.' The use of 'you' and 'your kids' directly addresses the viewer, implying that the consequences depicted (the overturned car) are a result of the viewer's parenting choices. This creates a direct address to the user, making them the subject of the implied causal relationship."@en ;
                :extractionMethod "llm_extraction_huggingface" ;
                :extractedFrom "74602.png" ;
                :extractionTimestamp "2025-12-19T12:17:03.821917" .

###  http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#toxicity_1
:toxicity_1 rdf:type :ToxicityAssessment ;
                rdfs:label "Harmful"@en ;
                rdfs:comment "The meme promotes corporal punishment as a necessary or protective measure to prevent negative outcomes, which is widely discredited by child development experts and public health organizations for its potential to cause physical and psychological harm to children. It also normalizes violence as a parenting tool, which can contribute to a culture of abuse and may influence parents to use physical discipline."@en ;
                :extractionMethod "llm_extraction_huggingface" ;
                :extractedFrom "74602.png" ;
                :extractionTimestamp "2025-12-19T12:17:03.821917" .

###  http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#parenting_ideology
:parenting_ideology rdf:type :TargetCommunity ;
                rdfs:label "Parents who adhere to punitive parenting ideologies"@en ;
                rdfs:comment "The meme targets parents who believe in or are influenced by punitive parenting ideologies that equate physical punishment (spanking) with effective child-rearing. It attempts to instill fear and guilt in these parents by suggesting that not spanking leads to catastrophic outcomes, thereby reinforcing their belief system or pushing them toward adopting spanking as a parenting tool."@en ;
                :extractionMethod "llm_extraction_huggingface" ;
                :extractedFrom "74602.png" ;
                :extractionTimestamp "2025-12-19T12:17:03.821917" .


